      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "The provider overrides parse with a cached snapshot. Selected-source\n reads must deliver and fingerprint that snapshot without fetching again;\n this trait-required callback rejects a refetch. Primitive provider reads\n are covered separately, and executing this guard would fail this test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Role and form:** the citation is a fenced `cite` on an explicit function with a body. The commentary is in the assessed input and gives a reason, not just difficulty.\n- **The explanation matches the source:** `CachedProvider` overrides `parse` and builds the result from an in-memory snapshot with `parse_snapshot`, so it never calls `read`. The default `defaults::parse` in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/sources.rs` would call `provider.read`, so a refetch would mean the override was bypassed. `read` returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"must not refetch\")`, so it is a guard that fails the test if it runs, as the commentary says. It is also required by the `SourceProvider` trait, so it can't be removed.\n- **Test scope:** the test script calls `search_structure`, `outline_source` and `read_source`. It never calls a text search, which is the other default path that calls `provider.read`. The claim that primitive provider reads are covered separately is plausible, since `LocalSources` has its own `read`.\n- **No other gap:** the item has no other citations. It has no redundant `impl` or `test` reference. A zero-execution exception is appropriate for a function that executing would make the test fail.",
